            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for an origin, then the Latin for great age."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: rēx, rēgis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rēx, rēgis — \"a king\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ōlim rēgēs hanc urbem tenēbant. Once kings held this city."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine, 3rd declension. After the last king was driven out, the Romans hated the very name rēx."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rēx is inherited from Proto-Indo-European h₃rḗǵs, \"ruler, king\" (EtymDB), a cousin of Sanskrit rāj-. English regal and royal come from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
